====================================================
WITHOUT ABSTRACT FACTORY (PROBLEM)
====================================================

Example:

GUI application supports multiple operating systems:

Windows:
- Windows Button
- Windows Scrollbar

Mac:
- Mac Button
- Mac Scrollbar


Without Abstract Factory, client creates objects directly.

====================================================
CODE WITHOUT ABSTRACT FACTORY
====================================================

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;


// Button Interface

class Button
{
public:

    virtual void render() = 0;

    virtual ~Button() = default;
};


// Scrollbar Interface

class Scrollbar
{
public:

    virtual void render() = 0;

    virtual ~Scrollbar() = default;
};



// Windows Products

class WindowsButton : public Button
{
public:

    void render() override
    {
        cout << "Windows Button" << endl;
    }
};


class WindowsScrollbar : public Scrollbar
{
public:

    void render() override
    {
        cout << "Windows Scrollbar" << endl;
    }
};



// Mac Products

class MacButton : public Button
{
public:

    void render() override
    {
        cout << "Mac Button" << endl;
    }
};


class MacScrollbar : public Scrollbar
{
public:

    void render() override
    {
        cout << "Mac Scrollbar" << endl;
    }
};



// CLIENT CREATES OBJECTS DIRECTLY

class Client
{

private:

    Button* button;
    Scrollbar* scrollbar;


public:

    Client(string os)
    {

        if(os == "Windows")
        {
            button = new WindowsButton();
            scrollbar = new WindowsScrollbar();
        }

        else if(os == "Mac")
        {
            button = new MacButton();
            scrollbar = new MacScrollbar();
        }

    }


    void render()
    {
        button->render();
        scrollbar->render();
    }


    ~Client()
    {
        delete button;
        delete scrollbar;
    }

};



int main()
{

    Client c1("Windows");

    c1.render();


    return 0;
}
====================================================
PROBLEMS WITH THIS DESIGN (WITHOUT ABSTRACT FACTORY)
====================================================
"Here the client knows every concrete class (WindowsButton, MacButton, etc.). If I add Linux support, 
I must modify the Client class, violating the Open/Closed Principle. This tight coupling is what Abstract Factory solves."

1. TIGHT COUPLING
-----------------

Client directly depends on concrete classes.

Client knows:

- WindowsButton
- WindowsScrollbar
- MacButton
- MacScrollbar


Problems:

- Client is tightly coupled with implementations.
- Any change in concrete classes affects client code.
- Difficult to maintain.



====================================================


2. VIOLATES OPEN/CLOSED PRINCIPLE
----------------------------------


Open for extension,
Closed for modification.


Example:

New OS support added:

Linux:

- LinuxButton
- LinuxScrollbar


Need to modify existing Client:


else if(os == "Linux")
{
    button = new LinuxButton();

    scrollbar = new LinuxScrollbar();
}


Every new product family requires changing existing code.


Problems:

- More changes
- More testing
- Higher chance of bugs



====================================================


3. OBJECT FAMILY INCONSISTENCY
-------------------------------


Client can accidentally create incompatible objects.


Example:


button = new WindowsButton();

scrollbar = new MacScrollbar();



Result:

Windows Button + Mac Scrollbar


Objects from different families are mixed.


Abstract Factory ensures:

WindowsFactory creates only:

- WindowsButton
- WindowsScrollbar


MacFactory creates only:

- MacButton
- MacScrollbar



====================================================


4. CREATION LOGIC MIXED WITH CLIENT LOGIC
------------------------------------------------


Client is responsible for:

- Deciding which object to create
- Creating objects
- Using objects


Example:

if(os == "Windows")
    new WindowsButton();


if(os == "Mac")
    new MacButton();



Problems:

- Client becomes complex.
- Object creation logic is scattered.
- Harder to reuse.



====================================================


5. NOT SCALABLE
--------------


For many platforms and products:


OS:

- Windows
- Mac
- Linux


Products:

- Button
- Scrollbar
- Checkbox
- Menu


Client code grows with many if-else conditions.


====================================================


INTERVIEW SUMMARY:

"Without Abstract Factory, client directly creates concrete objects.
This creates tight coupling, violates Open/Closed Principle,
allows incompatible object combinations, and makes the system
hard to extend."
